 "grammar": {
  "title": "Gerund or Infinitive",
  "explanation": "A **gerund** is the -ing form of a verb used like a noun. **Swimming** is good for you.\n\nAn **infinitive** is to + base verb. I want **to swim**.\n\nAfter **enjoy**, **avoid**, **finish**, and **mind**, use **-ing**. These verbs never take to + verb.\n\nAfter **want**, **decide**, **hope**, and **promise**, use **to + verb**. We decided **to act** now, before climate change gets worse.\n\nAfter a preposition, always use **-ing**. Fighting **climate change** takes time, and we are interested **in learning** more.\n\nSome verbs take a person + **to + verb**. My parents want **me to study** hard.\n\nA few verbs change their meaning with the form. I stopped **smoking** means I do not smoke now, but I stopped **to smoke** means I stopped in order to smoke.",
  "examples": [
   "*I **enjoy cooking**.* (enjoy + -ing)",
   "*We **decided to stay** at home.* (decide + to)",
   "*I **enjoy reading** before bed.* (enjoy + -ing)",
   "***Smoking** is not allowed here.* (-ing as the subject)",
   "*She's good **at drawing**.* (preposition + -ing)",
   "*He **promised to call** me.* (promise + to)"
  ],
  "practice": [
   {
    "q": "Correct the sentence: I enjoy to cook.",
